    "date_str": "2025-12-08",
    "description": "Will the response to Ditwah be worse than disaster itself?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Will-the-response-to-Ditwah-be-worse-than-disaster-itself/4-785346",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1765138961.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-08-dailyftlk-bdcb45c6-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "65105db9cca5a4a39d5d7ac5822d9538",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 889,
    "chunk_text": "While politically challenging, the Government must consider empowering the market\u2019s knowledge system rather than attempting to override it. Controlling an economy\u2019s vast array of prices, goods, and services is an informationally impossible task for any central authority, as evidenced by economic history worldwide and the experience of developed nations.\n\nSuch a framework of Government restraint would free capital, honour savers, and allow market prices to perform their vital role of allocating scarce resources efficiently. The unseen benefit would be a more resilient, adaptive, and prosperous society.\n\nOnly by accounting for the full consequences of policy\u2014both seen and unseen\u2014can Sri Lanka build a foundation for genuine, resilient recovery from Ditwah and future prosperity.\n\n(The writer, a civil engineer, resides in Singapore. He can be reached at [email\u00a0protected].)"
