    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f58a048286434eddb33852d6229acbb8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "The Prime Minister noted that Sri Lanka has now transitioned from the emergency response phase into the recovery and reconstruction phase. She emphasised that the Government\u2019s immediate priorities include the full restoration of essential services including electricity, water supply, transportation links, health facilities, and telecommunications across all affected districts, ensuring that communities can return to stability as quickly as possible.\n\nDisaster Management Centre Director General Major General Sampath Kotuwegoda presented an overview of the current situation in the affected areas, as well as findings from early rapid damage assessments.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority Chairman Buddhika Hewawasam together with representatives from the Sri Lanka Association of Inbound Tour Operators (SLAITO) and the Hotel Association of Sri Lanka (THASL) highlighted that key tourism sites including wildlife parks, marine parks, and cultural attractions have reopened and are accessible, and encouraged tourists to visit as overall tourism operations continue to stabilise. He further emphasised that prompt action was taken to evacuate all tourists stranded in the affected areas.\n\nThe tourism industry stakeholders emphasised that the strongest support the international community can offer Sri Lanka at this time is to continue choosing the country as a travel destination and requested the diplomatic community to encourage the regular review of travel advisories in line with improvements on-the-ground, which in turn will support the affected communities.\n\nRepresentatives of the diplomatic corps and international organisations present at the meeting reaffirmed their solidarity with Sri Lanka and briefed on both ongoing initiatives and planned support measures to assist the country\u2019s recovery and reconstruction efforts."
  },
