    "md5": "a487018a7c1500385ec859d6ff161463",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1618,
    "chunk_text": "The Commission also noted that offences that criminalise the dissemination of rumours that could cause \u2018public alarm\u2019 could result in unreasonable, unnecessary, and disproportionate restrictions on speech, the accuracy of which is contested. The Commission expressed concern that truth and falsehoods are not easy to establish, particularly in times of public emergencies, and the Government should, except in the case of false information that incites violence, refrain from attempting to control, through criminal sanctions, the flow of information purely on the basis of accuracy.\n\nAmend Regulation 8(1) to either exclude residential premises, or to include a guarantee that alternative housing or accommodation be provided to affected persons\n\nAmend Regulation 12 to permit the relevant Court the discretion in terms of sentencing and imposing any additional penalties\n\nRemove references to sections 365 and 365A of the Penal Code\n\nAmend Regulation 16(8) to ensure that the Human Rights Commission of Sri Lanka is notified of any arrest made under these Regulations within 24 hours of such arrest\n\nDelete any reference to the death sentence from Regulation 17(1)\n\nAmend Regulation 18(a) to remove all references to the term \u2018disaffection\u2019\n\nApply the ordinary law of criminal procedure with respect to the investigation of any offences under the Regulations\n\nComprehensively overhaul the template set of regulations that are used during public emergencies in consultation with relevant experts with a view to ensuring compatibility with fundamental rights and international human rights norms and standards."
  },
