    "md5": "a4a8b63d7ded9ad3a4aaf36e9b5a9cb0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1578,
    "chunk_text": "On another occasion he came with me to see my brother who was after a mild stroke to speak with him. My brother asked him how his wife was. Replied the monk with a broad smile \u201cI have no wife.\u201d He had renounced his all.\n\nBoth husband and wife were very Buddhist inclined and oriented. Finally, mutually consenting, they joined the Sangha \u2013 he as Denmarke Mettavihari and she as a fully ordained Bhikkhuni, residing in Kandy, often at Dhamma Kuta, beyond the University of Peradeniya. This step of total renunciation was taken in 1988.\n\nThere are few words that can be used to describe the excellence of his personality traits \u2013 extremely kind, compassionate human being, living by Vinaya rules to the letter and yet a very friendly, often given to humour. He was so easy to chat to and even pour out your hear and mind to him. I have, more than once, phoned him and gone over to the Narada Centre to get consolation from him for mental stress.\n\nHe introduced me to the Ajahn Brahm Society which he formed with a couple of devoted and modern-thinking young persons who invited and arranged the visits to Sri Lanka of Ajahn Brahmavamso and Ajahn Brahmali from Perth, Australia. Thousands of lay persons and hundreds of bhikkhus and bhikkhunis benefitted from their talks and medication retreats. Because I wrote a couple of articles for the Society, led by Ven Mettavihari, its members came to thank me with a huge cake!\n\nOnce I was unwell with flu and could not attend a meeting. The next day he walked all the way to my home to see me. That was a measure of his concern."
  },
