    "chunk_text": "A very valuable contribution of his was the digitalizing of the Tipitaka, the Pali Canon, on the initiative of Most Venerable Madihe Pannasiha Mahanayaka Thera, his preceptor. The Tipitaka is available for free download in three languages from his website metta.lk. One of his close Dhamma friends was late Venerable Dhammavihari Maha Thera, who was Professor of Pali and Buddhist Studies and one-time Editor of the Buddhist Encyclopaedia. They were both resident monks of the Narada Centre that was once called the Buddhist Information and Research Centre under the directorship of Ven. Dhammavih\u00e2ri Maha Thera. Ven. Mett\u00e2vih\u00e2ri assisted him in the preparation and production of the second edition of Buddhist Monastic Discipline authored by Ven. Dhammavihari. His heart was always open to help others and he was ever ready to use his technological know-how in the service of the Dhamma.\n\nWith birth follows ageing and final death. He was 80 years of age when he passed away. He could have lived longer but for his illness. Of this, the years he spent in Sri Lanka were more than he lived in his home country. For the services rendered by him it would have been most fitting for him to be granted distinguished citizenship. But I understand that his application for citizenship was rejected. However, his personality was such that he did not bear any grudge and continued with his noble work with all humility.\n\nHis death is a reminder that all we do are temporary and fleeting in the long run. Death is inevitable but when, where or how is unpredictable. Venerable Mett\u00e2vih\u00e2ri is no more but he lives in the minds of all who closely associated with this noble person. May his sojourn in Sansara be short because of the merit accrued by him and may he attain the bliss of Nibbana according to his wishes.\n\n\u201cWhen Justice Steps In: The Supreme Court Breaks the Cycle of Ragging Impunity\u201d\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will 