    "md5": "6d1e6116438102b77b7709e8d8d7b44f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1748,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s topography dictates global tea prices\n\nA leading tea industry expert says that Sri Lanka\u2019s internationally famed tea business operates on a clear three-tier system, where the altitude at which leaves are grown directly dictates their market role and price.\n\n\u201cLooking at the sales data of Forbes & Walker, elevation isn\u2019t just about geography \u2013 it\u2019s about economics,\u201d the expert says. \u201cEach tier \u2013 high, medium, and low-grown \u2013 has a completely different function in the global market.\u201d\n\nAt the top are the\u00a0high-grown teas\u00a0from misty regions like Nuwara Eliya. \u201cThis is the luxury tier,\u201d the expert notes. \u201cThe slow growth in cool air creates exquisite flavour and aroma, fetching top dollar at auction. It\u2019s our prestige export, but it\u2019s fragile being dependent on perfect weather and global demand trends,\u201d he says.\n\n\u201cThe\u00a0medium-grown teas, mainly from areas like Kandy, are the industry\u2019s reliable core. These teas are the versatile workhorses,\u201d the expert explains. They offer good character at a more accessible price and are essential for creating consistent blends. Their strength is stable demand while they\u2019re always working to be valued on their own, not just as a blending ingredient.\u201d\n\n\u201cIn the coastal plains,\u00a0low-grown teas\u00a0are all about scale. This is the volume engine,\u201d the expert describes. These teas are stronger and yield more. They\u2019re built for mass markets in places like the Middle East and Russia, competing on price and volume with other robust teas. Success here is measured in tonnes shipped.\u201d\n\nThe expert warns that all three tires \u2013 not only high-grown teas, now face threats from climate change . The path forward is equal focus on all three tiers."
  },
