    "chunk_text": "5. Shiral assumes that there is a liberal movement and a liberal class in Sri Lanka. The reality is that this country has never had such a movement or a class other than a very thin social stratum, especially in urban areas. It seems that many people misunderstand neoliberalism as an extension of liberalism. Liberalism has nothing to do with neoliberalism; conservatives developed that extreme economic ideology, because conservatism was devoid of a proper system and a theory related to economics to be consistent with their regressive political doctrine.\n\n7. There is another matter that Shiral and his colleagues must consider. The common name of their group is called Dinana Dakuna or the Winning South (\u0daf\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db1 \u0daf\u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dab).\u00a0 This is not the correct name for them, because there are multiple connotations of that term. What is the South; geographically demarcated south or non-northern south or the right-wing in the absence of the Left? What is the line of separating two entities?\u00a0 What are the criteria of selecting individuals, groups and parties into this entity called Dinana Dakuna?\u00a0The traditional Left/Right division is not valid in politics anymore, because all parties are qualitatively in the Right now. The JVP/NPP is also in the camp of the Right, but they are in the far Right.\n\n8. Shiral then turns to prescribe for the current problem, that is, the rule of the inefficient JVP/NPP government. He attempts to find a substitution through the amalgamation of four forces or their leaders. According to him, there are five leaders, i.e., Ranil, Dilith, Namal, Sajith and Milinda Moragoda, who is out of the scene now. He prefers Ranil despite this mature leader\u2019s declared poor health condition.\u00a0But the reality is that the SJB led by Sajith cannot be cornered, if anybody wants to defeat the incumbent government. Meanwhile, Shiral has overlooked the Harini-factor, which apparently causes damage to the coherence of the government."
  },
