    "description": "Ditwah disaster and the tax system: Relief for victims, recognition for donors",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Ditwah-disaster-and-the-tax-system-Relief-for-victims-recognition-for-donors/4-785752",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1765932041.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-17-dailyftlk-5c605e64-0005",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cbd80e53729260ab3b2127036c8a5962",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1400,
    "chunk_text": "Cyclone Ditwah has tested Sri Lanka not only in terms of physical resilience but also in terms of institutional responsiveness, social solidarity, and policy fairness. While the country has demonstrated remarkable unity and compassion, both from those directly affected and from those who voluntarily contributed to relief efforts, the existing tax framework has exposed certain gaps that warrant urgent attention.\n\nIn the aftermath of an unprecedented national calamity, tax policy must go beyond mechanical application and reflect principles of equity, reasonableness, and public interest. Allowing the carry forward of excess donations, enhancing deductions for Government disaster relief contributions, and adopting a pragmatic and empathetic approach in audits would not only bring fairness to the tax system but also strengthen public trust in revenue administration.\n\nUltimately, a tax system that recognises both the suffering of disaster victims and the selfless contributions of donors becomes an instrument of national recovery rather than merely a tool for revenue collection. Cyclone Ditwah presents a rare opportunity for Sri Lanka to reaffirm that taxation, at its best, is not just about numbers but about justice, humanity, and shared responsibility.\n\n(The author is a retired Deputy Commissioner General of the Inland Revenue Department.\u00a0 He can be contacted at [email\u00a0protected])"
